Question 3 — Follow-up

How would you handle a patient who has no documented progression, starts a new anti-cancer therapy, and then dies two months later? Would you count the death as a PFS event or censor the patient?

**Updated & Refined Version** (incorporating your correction):

---

For PFS in the ADTTE dataset, I would **not** automatically count death as an event. I would first strictly follow the study-specific SAP censoring rules.

In general, PFS is defined as the time from randomization (or first dose) to the earliest of documented disease progression or death from any cause. However, when a subject starts new anti-cancer therapy before documented progression or death, many oncology studies censor the subject at the date of the **last adequate tumor assessment prior to the new therapy**. In such cases, a subsequent death after the new anti-cancer therapy would typically **not** be counted as a PFS event, as the subsequent treatment can confound the disease course and interpretation of the endpoint.

Key variables in ADTTE include `STARTDT`, `ADT` (or `CNSRDT`), `AVAL`, `CNSR`, `EVNTDESC`, and `PARAM`/`PARAMCD`. The event date is derived from the earliest qualifying progression (e.g., RECIST PD from tumor assessment data) only when it qualifies under the SAP rules. Censoring is applied according to the SAP for scenarios such as new anti-cancer therapy, missing assessments, or data cutoff.

I always verify the exact rules in the SAP, as some studies include sensitivity analyses or regulatory-specific handling (e.g., differences in FDA vs. EMA preferences) for deaths after missed assessments or after new therapy. I implement the logic transparently with clear flagging and traceability, then validate through subject-level listings and independent QC to ensure the results are robust and submission-ready.


